// Appendix C — Example labs. One h2 per directory under examples/, each
// quoting its vmlab.wcl from the repository.

page apx_examples {
  title = "Example labs"
  chapter_header "Example labs" {
    kicker       = "Appendices · reference"
    reading_time = "16 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    The `examples/` directory of the repository holds ten labs and a set of
    template definitions. Each is a working `vmlab.wcl` with its scripts,
    and each exists to show one part of the product. This appendix says what
    every example demonstrates, what it needs before `vmlab up`, how to run
    it, and which chapters of this manual it illustrates. The lab files are
    quoted from the repository, so they match the code the manual was
    written against.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Every example runs from its own directory. Most name a
    [template](guide_templates) that you build first under
    `examples/templates`, and a few pull one from a
    [registry](guide_registries) on the first `vmlab up`. Guest credentials
    are the ones each template bakes in: `Administrator` / `vmlab123!` on
    Windows Server 2025, `vmlab` / `vmlab` on the Linux images.
  DOC

  h2 "ad-lab"
  p <<DOC
    A small Active Directory lab: a domain controller on a `corp`
    [segment](guide_networking) that hands out the DC as its DNS server and
    routes to a second subnet, a Windows 11 client that waits for the DC
    through `depends_on`, and an Ubuntu build box on its own NAT'd NIC pulled
    from a registry. It shows a static IP as a DHCP reservation, wave
    ordering, a [provision](guide_scripting), and two
    [event handlers](guide_events): a crash collector and a disk-low alert.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs `x86_64/windows-server-2025` and a pinned `x86_64/windows-11`
    in the [store](guide_templates); the repository ships a build for the
    first under `examples/templates` and none for the second. The Ubuntu box
    pulls itself on `up`, so its segment needs egress.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/ad-lab
vmlab validate
vmlab up
vmlab status
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/ad-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/ad-lab/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [The lab file](guide_lab_file), [Networking](guide_networking), [Guest automation with wscript](guide_scripting) and [Events and handlers](guide_events)."

  h2 "alpine-arm64"
  p <<DOC
    One Alpine Linux guest on an aarch64 machine, on a NAT'd segment with a
    host [forward](guide_networking) to its SSH port. On an x86 host there is
    no KVM for aarch64, so the VM runs under TCG emulation and boots in
    minutes; the [TCG warning](apx_troubleshooting) is expected here. The
    provision script waits for the [agent](guide_architecture) and logs the
    guest's `uname -m`, which should print `aarch64`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs `aarch64/alpine-3.23` in the store. That template's definition
    lives in the separate `vmlab-templates` repository, not under
    `examples/templates`; build it there with `vmlab template build`, then
    confirm it with `vmlab template list`. The host needs
    `qemu-system-aarch64` and the aarch64 UEFI firmware.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/alpine-arm64
vmlab up                          # TCG: give it a minute or two
ssh vmlab@localhost -p 12222      # password: vmlab
vmlab logs                        # the provision's uname -m line
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/alpine-arm64/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/alpine-arm64/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Your first VM](gs_first_vm), [Networking](guide_networking) and the architecture fields in [Lab file: vm and its children](ref_vm)."

  h2 "alpine-registry"
  p <<DOC
    A one-VM lab whose `template` is an OCI registry ref rather than a store
    ref. There is no build or pull step: the first `vmlab up` resolves the
    ref's tag, pulls that version into the store if it is absent, and boots.
    A cached version is reused without downloading again. With no tag the
    ref tracks the moving `latest`; `:latest-prerelease` tracks pre-releases
    and `:<version>` pins one.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs network egress from the host for the pull, and the segment
    declares `nat = true` so `apk` works inside the guest too. To make the
    next `up` pull again, remove the cached version with
    `vmlab template rm x86_64/alpine-3.23@<version> --force`.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/alpine-registry
vmlab up                          # first run pulls the template, then boots
vmlab status                      # TEMPLATE shows the registry ref
ssh vmlab@localhost -p 12222      # password: vmlab
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/alpine-registry/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/alpine-registry/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Distributing templates over registries](guide_registries) and [vmlab pull](cmd_pull)."

  h2 "dev-neovim-container"
  p <<DOC
    The second of the two worked dev-machine examples. A
    [lab container](guide_containers) running `alpine:3.22` in idle mode is
    the lab's [dev machine](guide_dev), with `./workspace` on the host landing
    at `/src` in the guest and syncing both ways. Neovim runs as a TUI over
    the [SSH facade](guide_logins)'s own session channel, so there is no
    client/server split and no marketplace. The `login "dev"` block declares
    the account alone, with no password, because the agent in a container is
    root and root needs no credential to become an account. That is the
    container identity floor.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    Its point is the guarantee both dev examples exist to show: a provision
    can write into the dev login's home before that user has ever logged on.
    `scripts/editor-bits.ws` does it with `dev01.as_login("dev")`, a second
    handle onto the same machine whose every call lands as `dev`. Without
    that line the same files land root-owned and Neovim's first run fails.
    The README records the durability rule too: what the provision places
    survives `down`/`up` and a restore, dies on `vmlab container destroy
    dev01`, and comes back on the next `up` because it is a declaration.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs nothing built. The image is pulled on the first `up`, the
    segment has egress for the package install and the plugin clone, and the
    host needs the container guest asset described in
    [Lab containers](guide_containers).
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/dev-neovim-container
vmlab up
vmlab status                      # dev01 ready, attachable
vmlab dev attach                  # waits for attachable, prints the alias, opens a shell
# in that shell:
cd /src && nvim hello.lua
./build.sh                        # writes /src/out.txt, which appears in ./workspace
# or straight off the alias:
ssh vmlab-dev-neovim-container-dev01 -t nvim /src
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/dev-neovim-container/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/dev-neovim-container/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Attach an editor to a dev machine](gs_dev_attach), [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev), [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins), [Lab containers](guide_containers) and [vmlab dev](cmd_dev)."

  h2 "dev-vscode-windows"
  p <<'DOC'
    The first of the two worked dev-machine examples, and the harder half.
    A Windows Server 2025 domain controller promotes itself to `PROBE`, and
    a second Windows VM joins the domain and becomes the lab's
    [dev machine](guide_dev), with `./workspace` on the host at `C:\src` in
    the guest. Two `login {}` blocks give it a domain user as the default
    [login](guide_logins) and an administrator, so `vmlab dev attach`, the
    facade's shell, its `sftp` subsystem and `vmlab exec` all land on one
    minted domain logon. That is what lets the build script read
    `\\dc01\team` from the editor's terminal with no credential prompt.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    The `corp` segment declares no `nat`, so the guest has no route off the
    segment. VS Code Remote-SSH still attaches, because the client-side
    setting `remote.SSH.localServerDownload: always` makes the client push
    the server over `scp`. `vmlab dev attach` and `vmlab ssh-config --print`
    print that snippet with the alias filled in. As in its twin, the point is
    `scripts/editor-bits.ws` placing editor bits into the domain user's
    profile through `dev01.as_login("dev")` before that profile exists. The
    README also records two riders §19.8 left open, each with the run that
    would confirm it.
  DOC
  p <<'DOC'
    It needs `x86_64/windows-server-2025` in the store, built from
    `examples/templates/windows-server-2025`. Two 4-vCPU, 8 GiB VMs boot,
    and the promotion and the join each reboot once, so the first `up` is
    long. Guest credentials are `PROBE\dev` / `vmlab123!` and
    `PROBE\Administrator` / `vmlab123!`.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
(cd examples/templates/windows-server-2025 && ./fetch-deps.sh && vmlab template build)
cd examples/dev-vscode-windows
vmlab up                          # dc01 becomes PROBE; dev01 joins and is provisioned
vmlab status                      # dev01 ready, attachable
vmlab dev attach                  # prints the alias and the VS Code settings snippet
code --remote ssh-remote+vmlab-dev-vscode-windows-dev01 C:\src
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/dev-vscode-windows/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/dev-vscode-windows/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Attach an editor to a dev machine](gs_dev_attach), [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev), [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins), [vmlab ssh-config](cmd_ssh_config) and [vmlab dev](cmd_dev)."

  h2 "mixed-lab"
  p <<DOC
    Windows Server 2025 and Ubuntu 24.04 on one NAT'd segment, plus an
    nginx [lab container](guide_containers) on the same segment. It exercises
    a static IP reservation, `depends_on` ordering across all three machines,
    an SMB [share](guide_shares) that appears on the Windows guest as `S:`,
    a segment [forward](guide_networking) to nginx on the Ubuntu box, a
    container `port {}` and healthcheck, a provision driving both guests, and
    a crash [handler](guide_events). With `gui = true` on the lab, `up` opens
    a [viewer](guide_vision) per guest.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs both templates built from `examples/templates`. The README
    explains one thing about the share worth knowing: the daemon maps `S:`
    as SYSTEM, so an interactive user opening it sees a credential error
    until they run the `vmlab-shares` script vmlab drops on the desktop,
    once per user.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
(cd examples/templates/ubuntu-24.04 && vmlab template build)
(cd examples/templates/windows-server-2025 && ./fetch-deps.sh && vmlab template build)
cd examples/mixed-lab
vmlab validate
vmlab up                          # winsrv, then nix01, then the web container
vmlab status
curl http://localhost:18080       # nginx on nix01, through the forward
curl http://localhost:18081       # nginx in the container
vmlab down                        # clones retained; vmlab destroy deletes them
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/mixed-lab/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Your first lab](gs_first_lab), [Networking](guide_networking), [Shared folders](guide_shares), [Lab containers](guide_containers) and [Events and handlers](guide_events)."

  h2 "peer-a"
  p <<DOC
    Side A of a cross-instance peering pair. Its `wan` segment is a
    [global segment](guide_networking) with a `connect {}` block naming the
    other side's trunk port, so the two [supervisors](guide_architecture)
    bridge the segment over a PSK-authenticated TCP [trunk](guide_networking)
    and `a1` here shares one L2 segment with `b1` over there. Both sides may
    declare `connect` at each other; the trunk table converges the mutual
    dial to a single trunk.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It pulls its Alpine template from a registry on `up`. Because the two
    sides address each other's `trunk_port`, they run under two supervisors:
    two hosts with the `connect` host changed to the other machine's address,
    or two users on one host, each with a different `trunk_port` and the same
    `psk` in their [host configuration](guide_host). Both supervisors run a
    DHCP server on the bridged segment, so leases can collide; the provision
    adds a deterministic secondary IP, `10.99.0.10` on this side, so the
    cross-trunk ping has a stable target.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/peer-a
vmlab up
vmlab exec a1 -- ping -c 3 10.99.0.20   # b1 on the other side
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/peer-a/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/peer-a/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates the global segments and trunks in [Networking](guide_networking) and the `trunk_port` and `psk` fields in [Host configuration file](ref_host)."

  h2 "peer-b"
  p <<DOC
    Side B of the same pair. It is the mirror of `peer-a`: the same global
    `wan` segment, a `connect` pointing back at side A's trunk port, and one
    Alpine VM whose provision takes `10.99.0.20` as its secondary IP. Run it
    under the second supervisor, with everything else as for side A.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/peer-b
vmlab up
vmlab exec b1 -- ping -c 3 10.99.0.10   # a1 on the other side
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/peer-b/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/peer-b/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates the same chapters as `peer-a`."

  h2 "riscv64-ubuntu"
  p <<DOC
    One Ubuntu 24.04 guest on a riscv64 machine, on a NAT'd segment with a
    host forward to SSH. Like `alpine-arm64` it runs under TCG on an x86
    host and boots slowly by design. The provision logs `uname -m`, which
    should print `riscv64`.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs `riscv64/ubuntu-24.04` in the store, built from the separate
    `vmlab-templates` repository, and on the host `qemu-system-riscv64`
    from QEMU 8.1 or later plus the riscv64 UEFI firmware, `qemu-efi-riscv64`
    on Debian and Ubuntu.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/riscv64-ubuntu
vmlab up                          # TCG: give it a minute or two
ssh vmlab@localhost -p 12322      # password: vmlab
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/riscv64-ubuntu/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/riscv64-ubuntu/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Networking](guide_networking) and the architecture and firmware handling in [Guest OS profiles](guide_profiles)."

  h2 "templates"
  p <<DOC
    Not a lab but eight `template {}` definitions, one per directory, each
    building an `x86_64` template into the store with `vmlab template build`
    run from that directory. Seven are Linux cloud images with a
    `cloudinit/` folder attached as a `CIDATA` [media](ref_vm) volume: cloud-init
    creates the `vmlab` user, installs the guest agent from the auto-attached
    VMLAB ISO, and disables itself for later boots. The script waits for the
    agent, which is the proof the install landed, and vmlab seals the disk.
    Ubuntu 24.04 uses the live-server ISO with a subiquity autoinstall
    instead, answering the installer's confirmation prompt through OCR and
    keystrokes. Windows Server 2025 is fully unattended from the Evaluation
    Center ISO through `autounattend.xml`, after `fetch-deps.sh` stages the
    virtio drivers and guest MSIs.
  DOC
  table {
    rows:
      | "Directory" | "Builds" | "Source" |
      | "`almalinux-10`" | "`x86_64/almalinux-10`" | "GenericCloud image, cloud-init. Needs an x86-64-v3 host CPU." |
      | "`arch`" | "`x86_64/arch`" | "Dated cloud image, cloud-init." |
      | "`fedora-44`" | "`x86_64/fedora-44`" | "Cloud Base image, cloud-init." |
      | "`opensuse-leap-16.0`" | "`x86_64/opensuse-leap`" | "Minimal-VM cloud image, cloud-init." |
      | "`opensuse-tumbleweed`" | "`x86_64/opensuse-tumbleweed`" | "Dated Cloud-Snapshot image, cloud-init." |
      | "`ubuntu-24.04`" | "`x86_64/ubuntu-24.04`" | "Live-server ISO, subiquity autoinstall." |
      | "`ubuntu-26.04`" | "`x86_64/ubuntu-26.04`" | "Cloud image, cloud-init." |
      | "`windows-server-2025`" | "`x86_64/windows-server-2025`" | "Evaluation ISO, `autounattend.xml`; 30 to 45 minutes; 180-day licence." |
  }
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
cd examples/templates/ubuntu-24.04
vmlab validate
vmlab template build
vmlab template list               # x86_64/ubuntu-24.04@24.04.4
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/templates/ubuntu-24.04/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/templates/ubuntu-24.04/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/templates/windows-server-2025/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/templates/windows-server-2025/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p <<DOC
    Each README says how to bump the version: update `version`, the image
    file name in `url` and `sha256` from the checksum file beside the image.
    The cloud images boot through SeaBIOS, which each file sets with
    `firmware = "seabios"` on the `linux-modern` profile.
  DOC
  p "Illustrates [Build a template from an ISO](gs_first_template), [Templates and the store](guide_templates), [Screens, input and vision](guide_vision) and [vmlab template](cmd_template)."

  h2 "winsrv-desktop"
  p <<DOC
    The smallest useful lab: one Windows Server 2025 VM on a NAT'd segment
    with its display surfaced on the host. Every VM runs headless and serves
    VNC on a Unix socket; `gui = true` makes `up` launch a
    [viewer](guide_vision) against that socket, and `vmlab console winsrv`
    attaches one at any time. Closing the viewer only disconnects, because
    the viewer is a separate process. On WSL 2, `vmlab console --tcp`
    bridges the socket to a localhost port for a Windows-side client.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    It needs `x86_64/windows-server-2025` built from `examples/templates`,
    and a viewer: the `viewer` field in host config, else `remote-viewer`,
    `gvncviewer` or `vncviewer` on `PATH`.
  DOC
  code sh { source = <<'SH'
(cd examples/templates/windows-server-2025 && ./fetch-deps.sh && vmlab template build)
cd examples/winsrv-desktop
vmlab validate
vmlab up                          # a viewer opens on the desktop
vmlab status                      # winsrv ready at 10.80.0.10
vmlab console winsrv              # reattach after closing the window
vmlab down
SH
  }
  code wcl {
    filename    = "examples/winsrv-desktop/vmlab.wcl"
    source_file = "../../examples/winsrv-desktop/vmlab.wcl"
  }
  p "Illustrates [Screens, input and vision](guide_vision), [vmlab console](cmd_console) and [Host configuration and WSL 2](guide_host)."
}
